> Elias Israel

What I said, indoor ranges with flexible target positionings.
Man-sized targets are good, too.

A few ranges offer "pop-up" man-like targets, in urban-like
situations, but these are mostly for the cops. I don't know of any
such ranges open to the public in the Bay Area, at least.

Rapid-fire is also not allowed at most ranges, for various reasons.
(Danger of uncontrolled shots, hits on neighboring targets, overall
noise, and the "negative atmosphere" that obtains when someone shoots
off an entire clip in a matter of seconds...mostly a market call by
the range operators. I've gotten permission to fire a number of
"double-taps": two shots in quick succession.)

Nor is quick draw practice allowed at most ranges, for similar
reasons. But that can be done at home, of course (without firing,
needless to say).

The main thing is avoid the one-handed, leisurely, bulls-eye practice
on targets at 25 yards.


-Tim May


