From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Stairway to Heaven (very tall towers)

> [ Assorted chat about high towers. ]

As an, er, stake in the ground, how high is the tallest existing tower?

> [ Dani Eder suggests a tower as KAO replacement. ]

I agree, 20 Km would be plenty for KAO-class astronomy.  Are you sure
about cost figures, though?  I expect the cost of the 747-as-KAO-replacement
is straightforward to determine, but it sounds to me like there would be
lots of non-recurring engineering costs to build the first really tall
tower, that might dominate the program.

How are present-day towers built, and does the construction protocol work
with 20 Km height?  (It might if they were built "top down", but might not
if they were built "bottom up".)  I am thinking that if the protocol
applied uniformly, one might guess that costs would be linear in total
tower mass.  Probably this is the kind of thing that civil-engineering
types tend to know.

What determines the upper bound to the height of towers that have already
been built; that is, why has no one gone to 10 or 20 Km already?  Based on
prior postings I suspect the answer has more to do with market and regulation
than with technology, but it would be interesting to know nonetheless.

                                          -- Jay Freeman

